"""Complete systemic sclerosis domains and begin original inflammatory myopathy questions."""
from create_rheum_sclerosis import rows,q,E,B,I,ref
MY=ref('BSR 2022 guideline for idiopathic inflammatory myopathy','https://doi.org/10.1093/rheumatology/keac115')
MC=ref('EULAR/ACR inflammatory myopathy classification criteria','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5736307/')
IBM=ref('Inclusion body myositis: diagnostic and therapeutic landscape','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9551222/')

q('A patient has progressive systemic sclerosis-associated ILD and needs an immunomodulatory treatment. Which drug is a commonly preferred option?', 'Mycophenolate mofetil',[
 ('Bosentan','Its vascular indications do not establish efficacy as ILD immunomodulation.'),('Nifedipine','It treats vasospasm rather than ILD inflammation/fibrosis.'),('Hydroxychloroquine alone','It is not an established sole treatment for progressive SSc-ILD.')],
 'Use an organ-specific treatment strategy','**Mycophenolate is an established immunomodulatory option for SSc-ILD.**',['Rituximab, cyclophosphamide, selected tocilizumab and antifibrotic treatment may be considered in appropriate settings.','Routine high-dose glucocorticoids are avoided because of renal-crisis risk.'], '**Choose by progression, severity and patient factors.**',['Monitor blood counts, liver/renal function and infection risk.','Discuss pregnancy prevention and alternatives when relevant.'],[['Mycophenolate','Immunomodulation'],['Nintedanib','Antifibrotic strategy'],['High-dose steroids','Renal-crisis concern']],[E,B])

q('A systemic sclerosis patient has severe reflux and esophageal dysmotility. Which intervention is most appropriate for acid suppression?', 'A proton pump inhibitor with appropriate lifestyle and positional measures',[
 ('Bosentan','It does not provide acid suppression.'),('Long-term NSAIDs','These may worsen gastrointestinal harm and do not correct reflux.'),('High-dose glucocorticoids','They are not standard acid-suppressive treatment and introduce other risks.')],
 'Treat reflux while recognizing the motility component','**PPIs are used for SSc-related gastroesophageal reflux and prevention of acid-related complications.**',['They suppress acid but do not restore normal esophageal peristalsis.','Assess dysphagia, weight loss, bleeding or refractory symptoms for complications or other pathology.'], '**Reduce late meals and adapt posture and intake.**',['Selected prokinetic treatment may address symptomatic motility problems.','Aspiration risk and nutritional impact deserve assessment when symptoms are severe.'],[['PPI','Reduces acid exposure'],['Dysmotility','Separate mechanical/functional issue'],['Alarm symptoms','Further investigation']],[E,B],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient with systemic sclerosis has bloating, diarrhea, weight loss and vitamin B12 deficiency. Which complication is most likely?', 'Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth due to dysmotility',[
 ('Isolated gastric antral vascular ectasia','GAVE explains bleeding/iron deficiency more directly than this malabsorptive pattern.'),('Uncomplicated acid reflux alone','Reflux does not adequately explain the lower-GI malabsorption syndrome.'),('A scleroderma renal crisis as the direct cause','The described GI syndrome is not a typical renal-crisis presentation.')],
 'Dysmotility can create a stasis-malabsorption cycle','**SSc small-bowel dysmotility predisposes to bacterial overgrowth.**',['The presentation can include diarrhea, bloating and nutritional deficiency.','Other causes of diarrhea, including medication effects, infection and malabsorption, remain relevant.'], '**Assess nutrition and use an appropriate antibiotic strategy when SIBO is established or strongly suspected.**',['Recurrent disease may need repeated or rotating treatment according to specialist assessment.','Severe distension or obstructive symptoms need evaluation for pseudo-obstruction and mechanical causes.'],[['Dysmotility','Stasis predisposition'],['SIBO','Bloating/diarrhea/malabsorption'],['Nutrition','Assess weight and deficiencies']],[E,B],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A systemic sclerosis patient has iron-deficiency anemia and endoscopy shows longitudinal red vascular stripes in the gastric antrum. What is the diagnosis?', 'Gastric antral vascular ectasia',[
 ('Portal hypertensive gastropathy','This more typically has a mosaic/snakeskin appearance, often in the fundus/body, in portal hypertension.'),('Mallory–Weiss tear','This is a mucosal tear associated with retching rather than this patterned vascular lesion.'),('Gastric lymphoma','This does not explain the characteristic striped vascular appearance.')],
 'Recognize the watermelon-stomach pattern','**GAVE can cause chronic occult blood loss or overt bleeding in systemic sclerosis.**',['Iron deficiency can occur even without dramatic hematemesis.','The endoscopic diagnosis should be linked to the anemia and other possible bleeding sources.'], '**Treat the bleeding and replenish iron.**',['Endoscopic ablation is commonly used when clinically indicated.','Acid suppression alone does not remove the vascular ectasia.'],[['GAVE','Antral vascular stripes'],['Portal gastropathy','Different pattern/context'],['Consequence','Blood loss and iron deficiency']],[B],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis has firm chalky deposits over pressure points with otherwise normal serum calcium. What is the most likely process?', 'Dystrophic calcinosis',[
 ('Metastatic calcification requiring hypercalcemia in every case','Dystrophic deposits can form in damaged tissue despite normal systemic calcium.'),('A urate tophus proven solely by white material','White material is not specific; crystal or pathological assessment can distinguish deposits.'),('Xanthoma from cholesterol deposition','This has a different lipid-related pathology.')],
 'Distinguish local tissue calcification from systemic calcium excess','**Dystrophic calcinosis occurs in damaged tissue without requiring hypercalcemia.**',['It can ulcerate, become infected and impair function.','Effective treatment is difficult and evidence for many medical options is limited.'], '**Assess pressure, wounds, infection and functional impact.**',['Selected surgery may help a symptomatic accessible lesion.','Do not promise that normalizing an already normal calcium will resolve the deposits.'],[['Dystrophic','Local damaged tissue'],['Metastatic','Systemic calcium/phosphate disturbance'],['Management','Symptoms, wounds and function']],[B])

q('For which systemic sclerosis setting may autologous hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation be considered?', 'Selected early severe diffuse disease with poor prognosis, after careful cardiopulmonary and other risk assessment',[
 ('Every patient with mild stable limited cutaneous disease','The treatment risk is disproportionate to that low-risk presentation.'),('Any patient with advanced severe cardiopulmonary failure regardless of transplant risk','Major organ impairment can make the procedure unsafe.'),('Isolated uncomplicated Raynaud phenomenon','This does not justify intensive transplantation therapy.')],
 'Balance potential disease benefit against treatment-related mortality','**Autologous HSCT has a role in carefully selected severe systemic sclerosis.**',['Selection requires experienced centers and detailed organ assessment.','Cardiac involvement is particularly important for conditioning-related safety.'], '**Do not equate a poor disease prognosis with automatic transplant eligibility.**',['Discuss alternatives and long-term follow-up.','The strategy is distinct from routine treatment of an isolated skin or vascular symptom.'],[['Candidate','Selected severe early diffuse disease'],['Screening','Detailed cardiopulmonary assessment'],['Risk','Treatment-related morbidity and mortality']],[E,B])

q('A woman has subacute symmetric proximal weakness, difficulty rising from a chair, a heliotrope rash and Gottron papules. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Dermatomyositis',[
 ('Inclusion body myositis','IBM more often has a slowly progressive finger-flexor/quadriceps pattern and lacks the characteristic DM skin findings.'),('Myasthenia gravis','Fatigable ocular/bulbar weakness with normal muscle enzymes is a different pattern and does not explain Gottron papules.'),('Polymyalgia rheumatica','Pain and stiffness predominate rather than this objective myopathy with characteristic rash.')],
 'A characteristic rash can identify the myositis phenotype','**Gottron papules and heliotrope change are characteristic dermatomyositis findings.**',['Assess objective strength rather than equate fatigue or pain with weakness.','Extramuscular disease includes dysphagia, ILD and cancer associations.'], '**Investigate the muscle and the systemic risk profile.**',['Measure enzymes and use autoantibodies, MRI, EMG or biopsy as appropriate.','Assess swallowing and breathing promptly when symptomatic.'],[['DM','Characteristic rash plus myopathic phenotype'],['IBM','Finger flexor/quadriceps pattern'],['PMR','Pain/stiffness without the same muscle injury pattern']],[MC,MY],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A 70-year-old man has 4 years of progressive asymmetric weakness, difficulty gripping tools, falls from knee buckling and mild CK elevation. Which pattern most strongly suggests inclusion body myositis?', 'Finger-flexor and quadriceps weakness',[
 ('Fluctuating ptosis and diplopia','This favors a neuromuscular-junction disorder.'),('Acute symmetric paralysis with areflexia after infection','This favors an acute peripheral neuropathy syndrome.'),('Pain-limited shoulder movement with preserved true strength','This favors a periarticular or pain syndrome rather than the characteristic IBM pattern.')],
 'IBM often differs from the usual proximal myositis pattern','**Deep finger flexors and knee extensors are commonly prominent sites of IBM weakness.**',['The course is usually slow and asymmetry is common.','Dysphagia can be clinically important.'], '**Correlate the pattern with EMG, imaging and muscle pathology.**',['Biopsy can show endomysial inflammation, rimmed vacuoles and protein accumulation.','An isolated antibody result is not a substitute for the clinical-pathological assessment.'],[['IBM','Finger flexors/quadriceps'],['Course','Slow and often asymmetric'],['Swallowing','Monitor for dysphagia']],[IBM],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Proximal weakness and very high CK persist months after a statin has been stopped. Anti-HMGCR antibodies are detected. Which diagnosis best fits?', 'Anti-HMGCR immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy',[
 ('Self-limited uncomplicated statin myalgia','Persistent marked weakness and CK elevation after withdrawal with this antibody suggest an autoimmune process.'),('Polymyalgia rheumatica','This does not usually produce marked CK elevation or this antibody association.'),('Steroid myopathy','This usually has normal or minimally elevated CK and a different exposure context.')],
 'Persistence after withdrawal suggests more than direct drug toxicity','**Anti-HMGCR-associated necrotizing myopathy can continue despite statin cessation.**',['Not every affected patient has prior statin exposure.','Muscle pathology often shows necrosis/regeneration with relatively limited inflammatory infiltrate.'], '**Refer for specialist-directed immune treatment.**',['IVIG and other immunomodulatory treatments may be needed according to severity.','Assess swallowing, respiratory strength and functional recovery.'],[['Toxic statin myopathy','Often improves after withdrawal'],['Anti-HMGCR IMNM','Can persist and require immune treatment'],['CK/strength','Monitor both']],[MY,ref('Anti-HMGCR myopathy: disease characterization','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5836404/')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Myositis, ILD, inflammatory arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon and mechanic’s hands suggest which syndrome?', 'Antisynthetase syndrome',[
 ('Primary antiphospholipid syndrome','A thrombotic/obstetric phenotype rather than this lung-muscle-hand pattern is characteristic.'),('Isolated inclusion body myositis','This does not usually produce the same systemic antisynthetase phenotype.'),('Classic drug-induced lupus','This does not best account for mechanic’s hands, myositis and ILD.')],
 'Recognize a lung-muscle-joint overlap phenotype','**Antibodies to aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases define an important myositis spectrum.**',['Anti-Jo-1 is the most familiar, but PL-7, PL-12 and others also occur.','ILD can dominate or precede obvious muscle disease.'], '**Assess pulmonary involvement even when weakness is mild.**',['Use HRCT and pulmonary function assessment as indicated.','Interpret antibodies with phenotype and assay reliability.'],[['Antisynthetase','Myositis/ILD/arthritis spectrum'],['Mechanic’s hands','Characteristic clinical clue'],['Anti-Jo-1','Histidyl-tRNA synthetase antibody']],[MY,I])

q('Which antibody is especially associated with clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis and rapidly progressive ILD?', 'Anti-MDA5',[
 ('Anti-centromere','This is more strongly associated with a systemic sclerosis vascular phenotype.'),('Anti-HMGCR','This is associated with necrotizing myopathy, usually with substantial muscle involvement.'),('Anti-Sm','This is an SLE-associated antibody.')],
 'A normal CK does not make the lung phenotype safe','**Anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis can have minimal muscle disease but severe lung involvement.**',['Cutaneous ulcers, palmar papules and systemic inflammation can provide clues.','Rapidly progressive ILD is an emergency.'], '**Assess oxygenation, HRCT and pulmonary function with urgency appropriate to the trajectory.**',['Do not wait for major weakness before considering the diagnosis.','Exclude infection while coordinating early intensive multidisciplinary treatment.'],[['Anti-MDA5','Amyopathic/ILD phenotype'],['CK','May not reflect lung severity'],['Rapid progression','Urgent treatment pathway']],[ref('ERS/EULAR CTD-ILD guideline: 2026','https://publications.ersnet.org/content/erj/67/1/2402533'),MY])

q('A patient with suspected inflammatory myopathy has patchy thigh muscle edema on MRI. How can MRI assist diagnosis?', 'Identify active muscle and guide a suitable biopsy site while being interpreted with clinical and laboratory findings',[
 ('Replace all clinical and histological assessment because edema is uniquely specific for autoimmune myositis','Edema can occur in other injuries and disorders.'),('Prove inclusion body myositis solely from any bright T2 signal','Pattern and pathology are important; edema alone is not diagnostic.'),('Establish irreversible fatty damage from edema alone','Edema and fatty replacement are different imaging findings.')],
 'Use imaging to improve sampling and phenotyping','**MRI can distinguish areas of edema from chronic fatty replacement and help target biopsy.**',['Avoid sampling only severely end-stage muscle when a diagnostic active site is available.','Recent procedures, exercise and denervation can affect signal.'], '**Integrate imaging with weakness distribution, CK, EMG and antibodies.**',['The safest and most informative biopsy site is selected clinically.','A nondiagnostic sample does not automatically exclude a patchy disorder.'],[['Edema','May suggest active injury'],['Fatty replacement','Chronic structural change'],['Biopsy targeting','Choose informative muscle']],[MY,IBM],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Which statement about CK in dermatomyositis is correct?', 'Normal CK does not exclude dermatomyositis, particularly amyopathic or selected hypomyopathic phenotypes',[
 ('Every patient must have CK above ten times normal','Enzyme elevation varies by phenotype and disease activity.'),('CK quantifies ILD severity directly','Muscle enzymes do not directly measure pulmonary injury.'),('Normal CK proves all weakness is psychogenic','Other myopathies, neuropathies and chronic damage can occur with normal CK.')],
 'Muscle enzymes are a tool, not an exclusion rule','**Some dermatomyositis phenotypes have little biochemical muscle injury.**',['Examine skin, objective strength and extramuscular disease.','Aldolase or other investigations can be informative in selected cases.'], '**Choose additional tests according to the phenotype.**',['Do not overlook anti-MDA5-associated pulmonary risk.','Distinguish active inflammation from damage, deconditioning and alternative causes of weakness.'],[['CK','Measures one aspect of muscle injury'],['Amyopathic DM','Characteristic skin disease without clinical weakness'],['Lung disease','Requires independent assessment']],[MC,MY])

q('A patient whose inflammatory myositis is improving develops gradual painless proximal weakness during prolonged high-dose glucocorticoid therapy. CK is normal and there is no new rash or pulmonary activity. Which alternative deserves particular consideration?', 'Glucocorticoid-induced myopathy',[
 ('Necrotizing autoimmune myopathy as proven by normal CK','Active necrotizing myopathy usually has a different biochemical pattern; normal CK does not prove it.'),('Myasthenia gravis as proven by a steroid prescription','The exposure does not establish a junctional disorder.'),('Acute rhabdomyolysis','This usually causes substantial enzyme elevation and a more acute injury pattern.')],
 'Treatment toxicity can mimic disease activity','**Steroid myopathy typically produces painless proximal weakness with little CK elevation.**',['Examine the trajectory and evidence of active immune muscle injury.','Deconditioning and chronic muscle damage may also contribute.'], '**Avoid reflexively escalating glucocorticoids for every new weakness.**',['Use objective strength assessment and targeted further testing when needed.','A supervised taper, steroid-sparing control and rehabilitation may be appropriate after evaluation.'],[['Active myositis','Seek objective inflammatory evidence'],['Steroid myopathy','Exposure plus proximal weakness, often normal CK'],['Management','Mechanism-directed adjustment']],[MY],style='clinical_scenario')

if __name__=='__main__':print(f'Sclerosis/myositis cumulative draft: {len(rows)} questions')
